  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM MDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nToday is a day that makes me feel a old (I am, I freely admit it). \nThis is the anniversary of my first operational meteorology job, at \na private weather company in Pennsylvania back in 2000. That's \nright, 26 years ago. It seems like yesterday. And the reason I am \nmentioning this is because the weather today will be similar to that \nday many years ago.\n\nBack on that early fall day, it was a nice day, with a cool morning, \na warm afternoon and a sunny to partly cloudy sky. And, with high \npressure will continue to control the weather across the area and \nbring a warm and dry day. Some of the warmer spots, like Greybull, \nWorland and even Casper could make a run at 80. But, there will be \none thing we didn't have on that day back in 2000. A dry cold front \nwill be approaching the area from the north. This will tighten the \npressure gradient somewhat and bring southwesterly breezes, \nespecially in the favored wind corridor from east of Rock Springs \nthrough Casper. Most of this area has a greater than 4 out of 5 \nchance of wind gusts over 30 mph this afternoon. This will raise \nsome fire weather concerns. However, humidity remains borderline. \nThe NBM ensemble mean low humidity generally remains between 16 and \n20 percent across this area, and would likely not be for 3 hours. \nSo, we will not issue any fire weather highlights. We will message \nelevated fire weather across the breeziest areas across the favored \narea though.\n\nThe front will move through portions of the area tonight and \ntomorrow, with similar conditions as today. The cold advection and \nnorthwest flow pattern will bring some gusty breezes to the northern \nBighorn Basin and especially northern Johnson County, with gusts \npast 30 mph. Fire weather concerns should decrease on this day \nthough. Cooler temperatures and somewhat higher humidity should keep \ncritical fire weather conditions away from northern Wyoming. Further \nsouth, wind will be lighter. Temperatures will drop about 5 to 10 \ndegrees on Saturday when compared to today East of the Divide, with \nareas West of the Divide having similar highs. Sunday looks like a \nnice day with above normal temperatures and a light to moderate \nwind. \n\nA shortwave moving in from the west will bring the only chance of \nshowers on Monday or Tuesday, but the chance is low with a capital L \nright now. There continues to be some differences in guidance in \nplacement of the showers and timing of the wave though. With \ndownsloping southwest flow ahead of the system, Monday will likely \nbe quite warm, with some many locations East of the Divide seeing \nhigh temperatures in the lower and even middle 80s. Much of next \nweek still looks have mainly dry, zonal flow across the area with \nflat ridging over the area. This means above normal temperatures and \nbreezy conditions at times. The main concern next week looks to be \nelevated to possibly critical fire weather, but confidence in \npinpointing a more concerning day remains low at this time. And for \nthose looking for cooler weather and possibly snow, you will likely \nbe waiting for a while longer.",
